"retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_or_modelling_newsvendor_model_ad233052_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Demand $D$ is 10, 20 or 30 with probabilities 0.2, 0.3 and 0.5. Each unsold unit loses $h=1$ and each unit of unmet demand loses $b=3$; $G(y)=h\\,\\mathbb{E}[(y-D)^+]+b\\,\\mathbb{E}[(D-y)^+]$ with $u^+=\\max\\{u,0\\}$. Which of the quantities 10, 20, 23, 30 gives the smallest $G$?","options":["$y=23$, with $G(23)=14$","$y=20$, with $G(20)=17$","$y=30$, with $G(30)=7$","$y=10$, with $G(10)=39$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"519e6b35b95e9896b40d1845","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_or_modelling_newsvendor_model_ad233052_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Demand $D$ is 10, 20 or 30 with probabilities 0.2, 0.3 and 0.5. A newsvendor stocks $y=20$ units, and sales equal $\\min\\{y,D\\}$. What are the expected sales?","options":["20, from $\\min\\{20,23\\}$ at the mean","23, the expected demand $\\mathbb{E}[D]$","16, from $0.8\\cdot20$ when $D\\ge20$","18, from $0.2\\cdot10+0.8\\cdot20$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"67e57a0375887382ac83473d","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_or_modelling_newsvendor_model_ad233052_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"In the newsvendor model an order quantity $y\\ge0$ is chosen once, before the random demand $D$ is observed. Units cost $c$ each, sell at price $p>c$, and leftovers are salvaged at $s<c$ per unit; $h=c-s$ and $b=p-c$. A planner simply orders the expected demand, $y=\\mathbb{E}[D]$. Why is this not the best choice in general?","options":["It ignores that a unit short and a unit left over cost different amounts.","It is always the best choice, since leftovers and shortages balance on average.","Demand is random, so no single order quantity can be called best.","It always orders too much, as some of the units will be left over."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a3ce7326f3e547cdf87bfff8","time_limit":50,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"}]}}